From: =?UTF-8?q?Lu=C3=ADs=20Duarte?= <lduarte@cloudflare.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 12:42:24 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Add per-paragraph auto base direction and first-strong lookup

parley can force a base direction (BaseDirection::Ltr/Rtl) but always
resolved Auto with a single whole-text first-strong detection. HTML's
unicode-bidi:plaintext (textarea/pre dir=auto) re-runs first-strong
per UAX#9 paragraph.

parley::BaseDirection { Auto, AutoPerParagraph, Ltr, Rtl } is a
superset of parlance's, set through the builders and PlainEditor as
before. AutoPerParagraph maps to Auto plus parley_engine's
AnalysisOptions::auto_per_paragraph, which splits at class-B
separators (CRLF = one separator), resolves each segment on a scratch
resolver, and splices levels back; the first paragraph donates the
layout-wide base_level, which only alignment uses; reordering is
per run.

HTML's dir=auto directionality is a DOM-side scan (it excludes bdi/
script/style/textarea subtrees and dir-attributed descendants), which
a text-stream resolver cannot express. The embedder walks text nodes
itself and needs only the per-chunk classification, so
parley::first_strong_direction exposes it: strong L is Ltr, strong R
or AL is Rtl, and anything else is None, so the caller keeps walking.
It classifies via the same parley_data properties analyze_text uses.

The analysis half and the classifier live in the parley_engine series,
so parley does not depend on parley_data.
